    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\nFollowing the existing pattern for computed-and-stored fields like `customerCode` and `householdId`:\n\n1. **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new function class) \u2014 a single-responsibility step that counts existing owners whose folded first+last name matches the built owner, and stamps the count on it. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the new owner isn't yet persisted and thus excluded from its own count.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired a new `countNamesakes` step into the pipeline between `assignHousehold` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbors.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it to the DTO by name automatically, and it's read back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` (readOnly int32) to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n\n5. **Schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreCountsNamesakes` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
